---
import CalculatorLayout from '@/layouts/CalculatorLayout.astro';
import MatrixCalculator from '@/components/calculators/MatrixCalculator';

const faqs = [
  {
    q: 'How do you multiply two matrices?',
    a: 'Each entry of the result is a row of the first matrix times a column of the second: multiply matching entries and add. For [[2, 1], [1, 3]] × [[1, 2], [0, 1]], the top-left entry is 2 × 1 + 1 × 0 = 2, and the full result is [[2, 5], [1, 5]].',
  },
  {
    q: 'When can two matrices be multiplied?',
    a: 'When the number of columns in the first equals the number of rows in the second. A 2×3 matrix times a 3×4 matrix gives a 2×4 matrix; a 2×3 times a 2×3 isn’t defined.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Is matrix multiplication commutative?',
    a: 'No. A × B and B × A are usually different, and one may not even exist. With the example matrices, A × B = [[2, 5], [1, 5]] but B × A = [[4, 7], [1, 3]].',
  },
  {
    q: 'How do I find the determinant of a 2×2 matrix?',
    a: 'For [[a, b], [c, d]], the determinant is ad − bc. For [[2, 1], [1, 3]] it’s 2 × 3 − 1 × 1 = 5.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Why doesn’t my matrix have an inverse?',
    a: 'Only square matrices with a non-zero determinant have an inverse. If the determinant is 0 the matrix is singular: its rows are linearly dependent, so the transformation can’t be undone.',
  },
];
---

<CalculatorLayout
  slug="matrix-calculator"
  title="Matrix Calculator – Multiply, Determinant & Inverse"
  description="Free matrix calculator for up to 5×5 matrices. Add, subtract and multiply matrices, and find the determinant, inverse, transpose, square or scalar multiple."
  intro="Add, subtract and multiply matrices up to 5×5, or find a matrix’s determinant, inverse, transpose, square or scalar multiple."
  faqs={faqs}
  updated="2026-09-27"
>
  <MatrixCalculator client:load />

  <Fragment slot="learn">
    <h2>How to use the matrix calculator</h2>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Choose an operation.</strong> Operations on two matrices show a second grid for B.</li>
      <li><strong>Set the size</strong> of each matrix (up to 5 × 5) and type the entries. Blank cells count as 0.</li>
      <li><strong>Read the result</strong>, which updates as you type. If the sizes don’t fit the operation, you’ll see why.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>Matrix rules</h2>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Operation</th><th>Requirement</th><th>Result size</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>A + B, A − B</td><td>Same size</td><td>Same as A</td></tr>
        <tr><td>A × B</td><td>Columns of A = rows of B</td><td>Rows of A × columns of B</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Determinant</td><td>Square</td><td>A single number</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Inverse</td><td>Square, determinant ≠ 0</td><td>Same as A</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Transpose</td><td>Any</td><td>Rows and columns swapped</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h3>Worked example</h3>
    <p>With A = [[2, 1], [1, 3]] and B = [[1, 2], [0, 1]]:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>A × B</strong> = [[2·1 + 1·0, 2·2 + 1·1], [1·1 + 3·0, 1·2 + 3·1]] = <strong>[[2, 5], [1, 5]]</strong></li>
      <li><strong>det(A)</strong> = 2 × 3 − 1 × 1 = <strong>5</strong></li>
      <li><strong>A⁻¹</strong> = (1/5) × [[3, −1], [−1, 2]] = <strong>[[0.6, −0.2], [−0.2, 0.4]]</strong></li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Inverse of a 2×2 matrix</h2>
    <div class="formula">[[a, b], [c, d]]⁻¹ = 1 ÷ (ad − bc) × [[d, −b], [−c, a]]</div>
    <p>Larger matrices are inverted with Gauss–Jordan elimination and determinants use row reduction, the same methods taught in linear algebra courses. To solve a single equation, try the <a href="/quadratic-equation-solver">quadratic equation solver</a>.</p>
  </Fragment>
</CalculatorLayout>
